    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the per-city capacity rule: creating an owner is rejected with **409 Conflict** when the owner's city already contains 50 or more owners.\n\nRather than duplicating logic, I reused the existing city-counting code and mirrored the established validator/exception/advice pattern already used for duplicate telephones and households:\n\n1. **`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 the service already had a private `countOwnersInCity(String)` (case-insensitive, used for customer-code sequencing). I exposed it on the interface and made the impl method `public @Override @Transactional(readOnly=true)`, so both `saveOwner` and the new validation reuse the single counting method.\n\n2. **`CityCapacityExceededException`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` mirroring `DuplicateTelephoneException`, documented as 409.\n\n3. **`CityCapacityValidator`** (new `@Component`) \u2014 mirrors `HouseholdDuplicateValidator`: holds the `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY = 50` constant and a `validate(Owner)` that throws when `countOwnersInCity >= 50`.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 injected the validator and call `cityCapacityValidator.validate(owner)` alongside the other create-time checks.\n\n5. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` mapping `CityCapacityExceededException` to a 409 `ProblemDetail`, consistent with the sibling handlers.\n\nEach piece has a single responsibility, and the count lives in one place. The provided acceptance test (`coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity`) and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
